WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.858 --> 00:00:05.444
This is a paced assembly line: five stations over a roller conveyor.

2
00:00:06.171 --> 00:00:09.828
Every takt, all carriers move on one station together,

3
00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:12.382
and each station does its share of the work.

4
00:00:13.392 --> 00:00:16.678
Customers want choice: another colour, a bigger motor.

5
00:00:17.402 --> 00:00:22.348
A variant is a version of the same product that differs in a few parts or steps.

6
00:00:23.066 --> 00:00:25.249
Here: small, medium and large.

7
00:00:26.275 --> 00:00:30.373
A mixed-model line builds them in a mixed order, product by product.

8
00:00:31.094 --> 00:00:35.808
That only works if the stations need no other parts or settings per variant.

9
00:00:36.902 --> 00:00:40.873
One line for all variants saves floor space and investment.

10
00:00:41.601 --> 00:00:43.540
But variants cost something too.

11
00:00:44.865 --> 00:00:48.161
If the stations do have to change, you build in batches,

12
00:00:48.336 --> 00:00:49.950
with a changeover in between:

13
00:00:50.124 --> 00:00:54.454
from the last good product of one variant to the first good one of the next.

14
00:00:55.224 --> 00:00:57.140
Meanwhile the line makes nothing.

15
00:00:58.166 --> 00:01:03.228
A shift has 420 minutes, and the line makes one product a minute.

16
00:01:03.977 --> 00:01:10.943
4 changeovers of 20 minutes cost 80 minutes: 340 products instead of 420,

17
00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.546
19 percent fewer.

18
00:01:13.553 --> 00:01:18.661
The batch size is the number of products of one variant between two changeovers.

19
00:01:19.657 --> 00:01:23.952
Bigger batches, 2 changeovers, give 380 products.

20
00:01:24.662 --> 00:01:29.271
The price: more stock, and a customer who wants another variant waits longer.

21
00:01:30.611 --> 00:01:33.711
On an automated line a changeover has two halves.

22
00:01:34.423 --> 00:01:39.613
The software half is the recipe: a stored set of settings per variant in the PLC,

23
00:01:39.694 --> 00:01:43.050
such as speeds, positions and robot programs.

24
00:01:44.073 --> 00:01:46.407
A wrong recipe is a classic mistake.

25
00:01:47.125 --> 00:01:51.084
So the operator scans the order, and the machine loads the right one.

26
00:01:52.113 --> 00:01:56.200
The hardware half is the change parts: parts that differ per variant,

27
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.010
such as these grip fingers.

28
00:01:58.998 --> 00:02:03.492
Good change parts are marked, sit on locating pins so they fit without adjusting,

29
00:02:03.631 --> 00:02:07.218
use quick clamps instead of bolts, and wait on a cart.

30
00:02:08.586 --> 00:02:12.092
Film a changeover of 50 minutes: 15 minutes searching,

31
00:02:12.197 --> 00:02:17.862
20 minutes swapping 6 change parts, and 15 minutes adjusting and running trials.

32
00:02:18.884 --> 00:02:23.818
With SMED, Shingo’s method, you split internal work, which needs the line stopped,

33
00:02:24.016 --> 00:02:27.546
from external work, which can happen while it still runs.

34
00:02:28.564 --> 00:02:30.282
Searching is external work.

35
00:02:31.052 --> 00:02:35.197
Set the parts out on the cart before the stop: 35 minutes left.

36
00:02:36.238 --> 00:02:40.383
Locating pins and quick clamps bring swapping down to 10 minutes,

37
00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:41.892
and adjusting to 5.

38
00:02:42.617 --> 00:02:43.662
15 in all.

39
00:02:44.361 --> 00:02:48.204
With two changeovers a shift, the line gains 70 minutes.

40
00:02:49.517 --> 00:02:54.068
On a mixed line each variant has its own work content at each station:

41
00:02:54.242 --> 00:02:56.193
the seconds of work on that product.

42
00:02:56.961 --> 00:03:03.660
At station 5: small 100, medium 120, large 160 seconds.

43
00:03:04.758 --> 00:03:11.167
The takt is 120 seconds, the mix 50 percent small, 30 medium and 20 large.

44
00:03:11.932 --> 00:03:16.797
A weighted average of 118 seconds: on paper it fits.

45
00:03:17.774 --> 00:03:21.129
But the operator handles each cycle as it comes.

46
00:03:21.867 --> 00:03:27.451
A large one is 40 seconds over takt; two in a row put him 80 seconds behind.

47
00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:30.824
A small one gives back only 20.

48
00:03:31.543 --> 00:03:34.864
He drifts into the next station, and calls for help.

49
00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:38.697
So the order matters as much as the mix.

50
00:03:39.443 --> 00:03:43.949
A sequencing rule: after every large one, at least two small ones.

51
00:03:44.718 --> 00:03:46.901
Then it stays at 40 seconds.

52
00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:52.455
A floater, an extra operator, helps wherever a heavy cycle runs over.

53
00:03:53.475 --> 00:03:58.943
In time, variant work moves to a sub-assembly: a side station that builds a module,

54
00:03:59.071 --> 00:04:01.358
which goes onto the line in one go.

55
00:04:02.366 --> 00:04:05.396
And there is no room at the line for every variant.

56
00:04:06.166 --> 00:04:10.740
So kits, or sequenced supply: parts in the same order as the products.

57
00:04:12.046 --> 00:04:16.307
Heijunka is levelling: building the mix and volume evenly over the day,

58
00:04:16.539 --> 00:04:19.755
in a small repeating pattern, instead of big blocks.

59
00:04:20.753 --> 00:04:24.793
The lesson’s example is a welding station for e-bike frames.

60
00:04:25.513 --> 00:04:30.424
Day demand is 180 A, 120 B and 60 C,

61
00:04:30.586 --> 00:04:34.568
with a takt of 70 seconds: a ratio of 3 to 2 to 1.

62
00:04:35.301 --> 00:04:38.679
On the line here they stand for small, medium and large.

63
00:04:39.688 --> 00:04:43.867
So: A, B, A, C, A, B, 60 times a day.

64
00:04:44.591 --> 00:04:49.363
A heijunka box fixes the pattern with kanban cards, a slot per time interval.

65
00:04:50.374 --> 00:04:52.975
If all the heavy C come at the end of the day,

66
00:04:53.195 --> 00:04:56.992
the welder falls 30 minutes behind: the daily overtime.

67
00:04:57.682 --> 00:05:01.862
With the pattern he runs at most 30 seconds over after one C,

68
00:05:02.036 --> 00:05:04.834
and the three A-frames after it win that back.

69
00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:08.628
But only if switching costs no changeover time.

70
00:05:09.934 --> 00:05:13.000
Once the order is fixed, suppliers deliver in it.

71
00:05:13.787 --> 00:05:19.104
That needs a frozen sequence: the last stretch before the line, often several hours,

72
00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:20.730
that no longer changes.

73
00:05:21.763 --> 00:05:25.803
A rush order pushed in breaks the supply, and gives missing parts.

74
00:05:26.795 --> 00:05:30.731
Flexible automation handles every variant without a changeover:

75
00:05:30.847 --> 00:05:32.971
servo axes that follow the recipe,

76
00:05:33.134 --> 00:05:34.562
grippers that fit everything.

77
00:05:35.585 --> 00:05:40.776
It costs more up front, but then every station builds every variant in any order.

78
00:05:41.522 --> 00:05:44.041
So ask first: what does switching cost here?
